5, 0.00913238525390625, 0.01776123046875, -0.018096923828125, 0.0261077880859375, 0.0011806488037109375, 0.00815582275390625, -0.00888824462890625, -0.007350921630859375, 0.005748748779296875, 0.0017137527465820312, -0.00949859619140625, -0.0216217041015625, -0.002132415771484375, -0.00656890869140625, -0.03857421875, 0.0103912353515625, -0.00811767578125, -0.01226043701171875, 0.003124237060546875, -0.0251312255859375, 0.0018138885498046875, 0.004955291748046875, 0.040740966796875, 0.0160064697265625, 0.01436614990234375, 0.020599365234375, -0.0220184326171875, 0.0496826171875, 0.05084228515625, 0.01861572265625, 0.0109710693359375, -0.001010894775390625, -0.059356689453125, -0.007167816162109375, 0.0238800048828125, 0.03167724609375, 0.017669677734375, 0.0303497314453125, -0.0016698837280273438, -0.0112762451171875, -0.01149749755859375, -0.01049041748046875, -0.00537872314453125, -0.043548583984375, -0.005527496337890625, -0.002056121826171875, -0.00920867919921875, -0.021087646484375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Democracy can be defined in various ways, and this document aims to clarify the distinction between minimalist and maximalist definitions of the term. At its core, democracy is a system of government in which political power resides with the people of a state. A minimalist definition, as articulated by scholars such as Joseph Schumpeter, focuses on the procedural aspect: democracy is characterized by the election of rulers through regular, competitive elections. In contrast, maximalist definitions, advocated by theorists like Robert Dahl, expand the concept to include not only competitive elections but also the protection of civil liberties and human rights\u2014such as freedom of speech, freedom of assembly, and equal protection under the law. The distinction between these definitions is significant, as some critics argue that the minimalist approach overlooks important substantive elements of democracy, while maximalist definitions may be criticized for being too broad or difficult to measure. Debates continue over which definition best captures the essence of democracy, highlighting the complexity and contested nature of the concept. Recognizing these differences is crucial for understanding how democracy is practiced and evaluated around the world."}}